WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.960
I'm several hundred feet up in the air. Up here I might encounter perhaps a flying insect,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.480
now I haven't seen one yet, or maybe even a baby spider clinging to a gossamer thread

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:35.360
of silk which is their way of getting around. But by and large this is the kingdom of the birds.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:48.200
The first birds flew about 150 million years ago. They spread around the globe and evolved

00:00:48.200 --> 00:01:00.040
into a multitude of different kinds. Aerial acrobats, stealthy hunters,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.000
and some of the fastest creatures on the planet.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Their extraordinary skill enabled them to surpass Earth's original fliers, the insects.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:28.640
But there is a vast kingdom that the birds do not control, the night skies. These are ruled by very

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:41.800
different creatures, flying mammals, bats. And in one spectacular place, these two populations

00:01:41.800 --> 00:02:00.800
of the night and the day collide.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:28.720
This is Segovia in central Spain. Some of the inhabitants of this gorge allow us to see very

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.720
clearly how birds as a group have become so versatile in the air through the ability to

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:42.080
change the shape and the size of their basic flying mechanism, the wing. And there's a wonderful

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:51.120
example of that just over here. You may think that birds are much the same when it comes to

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:59.640
flight, but in fact different species need to fly in their own particular way. This vulture is an

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:10.320
airborne scavenger. It feeds on the bodies of dead animals. So it needs to spot any fresh carcass

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:21.440
very quickly and get to it before others claim it. Like most birds, it has superb eyesight. So it

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:32.520
climbs high in the sky, constantly scanning the ground below for hours at a time if need be. To

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:44.080
fly in this highly specialized way, it has evolved a very distinctive kind of wing. To get up close

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.640
to some of the many vultures that live in this area, I'm visiting a place where they're regularly

00:03:49.640 --> 00:04:00.680
fed by conservationists. These are griffon vultures, one of the largest of all vulture

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:11.760
species. Each one can weigh up to 11 kilos. Lifting an 11 kilo body high in the sky takes a lot of

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:23.320
energy, but the vultures don't supply that energy directly themselves. A clue of how they do so comes

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:33.560
from observing their behavior at the start of the day. Those vultures roost and nest on ledges up

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:44.960
there. They're not early risers. That's because they rely on the sun to get airborne. As the day

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:52.200
warms up, patches of bare rock reflect the heat of the sun, forming columns of rising hot air known

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:58.360
as thermals. And the vultures know exactly how to exploit those thermals to be carried high in the

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:14.840
sky with the minimum of effort. They have wings that have been shaped over millions of years to

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:24.520
catch as much of that rising air as possible. They're huge, very broad, with a span of over

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:49.240
two meters. But riding thermals may not be as easy as it looks. A thermal is quite a narrow column

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:56.000
of rising air, and to stay within it, a vulture has to make quite sharp turns, and that can lead

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:04.880
to disaster. In a tight spiral, a vulture's inside wing travels a shorter distance than its outer wing.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:11.720
And if we were to measure the speed of this inner wing, we would find that it moves much more slowly

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:19.320
through the air. This means it generates less lift, so little, in fact, that the vulture could easily

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:29.040
stall and drop from the sky. It avoids that by having special control over the feathers at the

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:40.720
ends of its wings. They can be splayed so that they separate. As a result, each feather acts as a small

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:48.640
extra wing, and together they increase overall lift. This enables the vulture to turn in a

00:06:48.640 --> 00:07:01.720
tight circle, and so hold its place in a thermal and soar upwards. Using this technique, a vulture

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:07.880
can climb to a height of a kilometer above the ground with scarcely a flap of its wings. And then,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.960
if it spots food down below, it can switch its flying technique and descend at speed.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:32.760
A dead animal, once found, makes an easy meal, except for the fact that there are rather a lot

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:41.120
of animals that are too fast and dinous. But other meat-eating birds have even greater problems.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:50.680
They pursue living prey, and one such hunter has become one of the fastest of all living creatures.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:08:07.400
To watch one of the fastest of all flying animals, I've come to Italy and the city of Rome.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:17.640
There's a bird that flies over these roofs that finds its prey not on the ground,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:25.520
but in the air, and it owes its success to its speed. In fact, it's said to be the fastest

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:39.080
moving animal on earth, the peregrine. Peregrines hunt other birds. Many different kinds of birds

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.840
now live in cities, attracted by the food and shelter that is so easily found here.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:56.240
And a tall building like this is an ideal lookout for a hunter. Flying prey can move

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.400
in any direction it chooses, so a hunter has to be both fast and agile if it's to get a meal.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:17.320
A peregrine's wings have a very special shape. They're pointed and swept back. If wings have

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:25.440
a blunt end, air will swirl over that end, forming trails of turbulence. These act like

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:35.520
brakes, slowing a bird down. But pointed wings shrink that edge and so reduce the turbulence.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:43.480
Pulling the wings back towards the body makes the bird even more streamlined. And speed is

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:53.520
crucial to a peregrine's success. It also has acute vision that enables it to spot prey over

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:03.840
a mile away. And for the peregrines that hunt in Rome, these birds are prime targets. Starlings.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:15.880
They too are fast fliers, and their smaller size makes them even more maneuverable.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:29.760
So to catch a starling, a peregrine must be even faster. And in order to gain speed and

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:46.680
surprise, it attacks from above. First, it climbs. When it sees a group of its potential prey,

00:10:46.680 --> 00:11:11.920
it turns, dives, and accelerates by beating its wings. The starlings are still unaware

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:24.840
of the danger hurtling towards them. Finally, the peregrine draws its wings back. This is

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:34.040
called the stoop, a superb streamlined shape that slices through the air. Now it can reach

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:43.440
speeds of over 200 miles an hour. As it nears its target, it opens its wings to slow its

00:11:43.440 --> 00:12:12.360
descent and makes its final lunge. Starlings, in fact, are an abundant source of food for

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:18.360
the peregrines. They come into the city in the winter, attracted no doubt by the warmth

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:27.000
in order to roost. Every evening at dusk, the starlings start to arrive. And they have

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:34.960
a remarkable way of defending themselves against peregrines. One that relies on their ability

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:45.480
to fly together in tight formations as a flock. And here they come, vast numbers of them,

00:12:45.480 --> 00:13:12.480
tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands. It's like a great black hailstorm, a blizzard

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:26.040
storm. And now some start to fly closer together and perform far more complex maneuvers. Look

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:33.240
how these great flocks come together, form a cloud, veer away, then split. It's a quite

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:43.760
extraordinary piece of aerial navigation. We're still unsure exactly why they perform

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:50.320
these elaborate dances, but they're often triggered by the arrival of a predator. And

00:13:50.320 --> 00:14:10.880
today is no exception. Because over there, one of those buildings, I've seen a peregrine.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:16.120
Coming in in great numbers like this is in itself a defense, because if you're surrounded

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:20.680
by tens of thousands of others, well, it's a good chance that the peregrine won't get

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:33.720
new. But the aerial ballet is part of a more complex defensive strategy. When a peregrine

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:39.400
does attack with its wings drawn back in its stoop, the starlings, flying in their tight

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:47.160
formation, coordinate their escape. Instead of scattering in different directions, when

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:51.800
a straggler might be picked off, they stick together, even when they make the sharpest

00:14:51.800 --> 00:15:00.560
of turns. Recent studies analyzing the flight paths of these Roman flocks have now revealed

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:10.200
how they manage to do this. Each individual starling is constantly tracking the movement

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:17.680
of up to seven of its close neighbors, even if they drift far apart. This is a hidden

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:28.280
glue binding the flock together. And it can also help the birds to communicate among themselves.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:34.360
A bird that turns to evade a predator triggers a ripple that passes through the overlapping

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:51.540
networks in milliseconds. And that causes the whole flock to turn as one. As the light

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:58.720
finally fades, the flock suddenly descends into the trees that will be their boost for

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:04.800
the night. The peregrine's sharp eyesight doesn't operate nearly so well in the dark.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:17.640
So now the starlings are safe. Until tomorrow, that is. 6,000 miles away in South America,

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:25.200
there are other birds with a very different skill. And they also find their food on the

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:34.720
wing. In the cloud forests of Ecuador, there is a plentiful supply of a type of food produced

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:47.160
by plants to attract flying animals. Nectar. Around 130 million years ago, plants recruited

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:53.320
insects to transport pollen from one flower to another by bribing them with a sugar-rich

00:16:53.320 --> 00:17:01.400
drink. Birds, when they first evolved, were unable to collect it because there was seldom

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:10.720
something solid nearby on which they could perch. Then, around 30 million years ago,

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:35.500
a kind of bird appeared that had no need of such a perch. Hummingbirds. They could hover.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:41.360
They do so by beating their wings extremely swiftly, so fast, in fact, that they make

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:57.220
a humming noise. The largest hummingbird beats its wings around 14 times a second, but some

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:17.920
tiny species are able to do so 80 times a second. To fly in this extraordinary way,

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:23.760
hummingbirds have changed the structure of their wings and the way they beat them. Here

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:34.120
in Ecuador, scientist Doug Alpshula is working to analyse exactly how they do so. Hummingbirds

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:39.360
are remarkable animals. They have extreme adaptations in physiology and anatomy, and

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:44.280
they also have a very unique behaviour. They can hover. And the approach that we've taken

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:49.760
is to study how those physiological and anatomical adaptations determine their hovering ability.

00:18:49.760 --> 00:19:04.760
Using high-speed cameras, he records the mechanics of their flight in minute detail. He can slow

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:15.680
down the action by around 40 times and so observe exactly what's taking place. Most

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:24.640
birds flap their wings up and down, but hummingbirds flap theirs more like insects. They twist

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:30.200
their wings around between strokes and so can generate lift when flapping both forwards

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:46.120
and backwards. Doing this at high speed puts a huge strain on their wings. So to withstand

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:53.160
it, the wings have a special structure. The hummingbird wing is very stiff and undergoes

00:19:53.160 --> 00:20:07.480
few changes in shape as it rapidly beats back and forth. They owe this stiffness to

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:16.720
a modification of the bones. The arm bones have shrunk, but the bones of the hand have

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:24.800
elongated and support most of the wing's surface. Twisting this wing at the shoulder and at

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:38.000
the wrist produces the hummingbird's distinctive wing beat. Doug is also investigating one

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:48.240
of the great mysteries of hummingbird flight, their ability to move sideways in mid-hover.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:52.000
Hummingbirds are able to track flowers that are moving back and forth in the wind, and

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:57.720
this was something I always wanted to know more about. To replicate the swaying motion

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:08.520
of a flower, Doug places a reservoir of nectar on a mechanical slider. Before long, he has

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:23.240
a volunteer. Amazingly, it manages to track sideways to keep up with the slider and still

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:31.040
feed. The bird is exploiting an unexpected feature of its wing beat, not the flapping

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:37.640
itself, but the twist at the end of each stroke. During hovering flight, as the wings come

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:43.400
forward, they rotate symmetrically, so the forces remain in balance. But if they instead

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:48.320
rotate differently, such that one wing rotates before the other, then the forces are no longer

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:57.400
in balance, and this asymmetry can be sufficient to push them to one side or the other. So,

00:21:57.400 --> 00:22:03.040
a combination of modified wing bones and precise control of wing motion gives hummingbirds

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:10.720
the aerial agility they need to collect nectar. And they need plenty of it. Hovering burns

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:18.560
a huge amount of fuel. All hummingbirds have to constantly top up their tanks with high

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:30.600
energy nectar. And when supplies are low, competition can be fierce. Now, their flying

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:46.240
skills are put to a very different use. To fight off rivals.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:23:15.840
So, different birds adapted their wings to fly in highly specialized ways.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:24.080
Some began to hunt the Earth's first fliers, the insects. And in that battle, there is

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:42.440
now no real contest. But because most birds rely so much of their success on their exceptional

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:49.000
eyesight, there is one major habitat that is largely closed to them. Not a place, but

00:23:49.000 --> 00:24:03.800
a time. The night. In the British countryside, however, there is a bird that can fly in the

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:26.760
dark. And it's a very skillful hunter. The barn owl is one of only a very few birds that

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:35.920
can hunt at night. And one of its favorite meals is a field mouse. But first, it has

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:50.040
to find it in the dark. A mouse is extremely alert to the approach of a predator. But the

00:24:50.040 --> 00:25:16.360
barn owl has wings specially adapted for stealth. And senses that can penetrate darkness.

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:21.720
Its eyes are very sensitive in low light. But even if the mouse is out of sight, it's

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:30.560
still not safe. The owl's hearing is also very acute. Those two discs on its face channel

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:37.600
sound into its two ears, which are on a slightly different level on the head. And that difference

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:42.920
enables the bird to pinpoint the source of the sound, whether it's in the air or down

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:51.440
on the ground. But in order to hear that sound, its wing beats have to be very, very quiet.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:26:17.880
And the way it achieves that, we can see when it goes hunting. The key reason for its silent

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:25.920
flight lies in the nature of its wing feathers. Along the back edge, their fringe is frayed

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:34.560
and tatty. Most birds wings have a hard edge, and this can cause quite a loud noise. The

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:41.240
source is turbulence produced when air flowing over the wing rubs against its surface. When

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:49.680
this swirling air meets a hard back edge, the sudden drop off hugely amplifies the noise.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:56.160
But the barn owl's tatty feathers avoid that. By creating a softer edge, they cushion the

00:26:56.160 --> 00:27:14.200
turbulent air and so reduce noise. So silent flight allows the owl to hear its prey and

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:25.400
conceals its approach. But to position itself for the kill, it needs to fly extremely slowly.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:40.240
And to achieve that, it has particularly broad wings. This slow, silent approach leaves

00:27:40.240 --> 00:28:09.120
the field mouse little chance of escape. On nights when there is thick cloud or no moon,

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:21.040
even an owl's sensitive eyes struggle. But there are creatures that have such highly

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:29.560
specialized senses that they're able to navigate in total darkness. Among insects, there are

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:39.600
some moths whose elaborate antennae are able to pick up the sense of food or a mate. And

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:45.000
there are those nocturnal animals, the last group of flying creatures to appear on earth,

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:52.920
the bats. To see how they battle with the insects for dominance of the night skies,

00:28:52.920 --> 00:29:15.160
we're heading into the rainforests of Borneo. Many bats find their food not by sight or

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:44.920
smell but by using a very different and highly advanced guidance system. This

00:29:44.920 --> 00:30:11.240
is Domantong. The cave is a vast network of underground tunnels and cathedral-sized caverns.

00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:22.040
It was carved out by streams of water over millions of years. And now it's home to a

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:39.680
remarkable community of cave-dwelling specialists. To find the creatures I'm looking for, I'm

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:45.280
being winched high up towards the ceiling, where the towering walls make ideal roost

00:30:45.280 --> 00:31:02.120
sites for flying animals. These little birds flying past me are swiftlets that have made

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:09.840
their nests on the walls of the cave. They are active during the day and they leave the

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:18.560
cave to hunt insects. The bats that I'm interested in are farther behind me in the semi-darkness

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:25.800
and they're asleep now during the day. The bats are scarce to the size of mice. Their

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:34.560
wings are constructed with very long fingers and they hang by their feet from the rock.

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:44.920
Although there are a few at the back there, deeper in this cave they exist in huge numbers.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:54.200
To find their roosts, we're heading still deeper into Domantong cave. A huge black dune

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:59.800
fills the back of one of the main chambers. It's made up entirely of the droppings of

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:11.120
the vast numbers of bats hanging above in the darkness. To see them fly, we must wait

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:23.260
for dusk. The great tropical island of Borneo contains many huge caves and one of the biggest

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:32.040
is Domantong. High on the rocky cave ceiling above me, hidden in the darkness, there are

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:38.360
vast numbers of bats. You can get some idea of how many there must be because of this

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:47.240
huge dune behind me. That's formed of their droppings and if you see a little moving glint

00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:53.880
on the surface, that comes from an army of cockroaches which are chewing their way through

00:32:53.880 --> 00:33:05.120
the back droppings to extract the last particles of nutriment. Some people think there are

00:33:05.120 --> 00:33:17.800
a million bats up here in this cave. It's impossible to see them in the gloom but special

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:28.840
night vision cameras can reveal them, densely packed crowds hanging from the ceiling. Their

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:36.080
tiny eyes are adapted to low light but they cannot penetrate the blackness. Millions of

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.720
years ago, however, these bats evolved an extraordinary guidance system known as echolocation

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:54.120
or sonar. A bat produces extremely high pitched sounds in its throat and then projects them

00:33:54.120 --> 00:34:02.080
forward. We have slowed the sounds down but can still only hear them by converting them

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:09.640
to lower frequencies. They bounce off the walls as echoes and are detected by the bats'

00:34:09.640 --> 00:34:17.960
huge ears. These are in constant movement and enable the bat to map its surroundings

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:35.040
with remarkable precision. But these bats not only need to find their way in the dark,

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:45.920
they also need to find their food. Night flying insects. And among them are moths. Locking

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:54.120
on to these moving targets is a supreme test for the bats' echolocation system. As one

00:34:54.120 --> 00:35:11.720
homes in, its sonar beam switches into attack mode, increasing the rate of its pulses. This

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:19.880
enables it to precisely pinpoint the location of its prey. But the bats don't have it all

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:29.040
their own way in the darkness. A team of scientists in Borneo has discovered that there is another

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:41.680
creature here that uses sound as a weapon. This tent acts as an enclosed flight of bats

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:49.280
arena in which every movement and sound can be recorded in minute detail. With these cameras

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:54.800
and ultrasonic microphones we can see how these interactions unfold and hear how they

00:35:54.800 --> 00:36:05.520
unfold. The team play recordings of bat sounds to moths. This reveals a remarkable ability.

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:11.840
Here in Borneo we recently discovered that hawk moths respond to these echolocation cries

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:24.200
with their own sounds. Hawk moths do it with the tips of their abdomen, with modified genitals.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:31.680
They rub the genitals against the inside of the abdomen and reply to this bat attack.

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:37.880
The moth is tethered to keep it in range of the cameras and microphones. Then a bat is

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:56.120
released. As the bat approaches the moth, its sonar pulse switches to attack mode. But

00:36:56.120 --> 00:37:04.520
now the hawk moth responds, sending its own rasping sound back with astonishing effect.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:11.240
At the last moment the bat appears to lose track of the moth and fails to catch it. We've

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:17.980
shown that these moth sounds actually jam the bat's sonar. They interfere with the returning

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:27.240
echoes from the insect and causes the bat to miss the moth. The team has discovered

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:36.040
that insects are fighting back in the ongoing battle for the night skies. But there are

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:43.760
of course plenty of other flying insects with no such defences. And they live in vast numbers

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:54.040
in the forests outside Gomantong Cave. So every evening as dusk arrives, the bats leave

00:37:54.040 --> 00:38:05.840
the safety of their secluded home to hunt. And now the bats are beginning to use their

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:12.120
echolocation skill to fly out from their roosts in the depths of the cave, coming close to

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:24.480
the ceiling and then whizzing out through this little entrance here. They don't collide

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:28.640
with the roof, they don't collide with one another or even with me. All through that

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:45.360
echolocation, there they go. But this is just a trickle. The main exodus is taking place

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:55.400
up a chimney that's deeper in the cave. To watch close up the way the bats achieve their

00:38:55.400 --> 00:39:01.160
million strong mass departure, I'm being hauled up 200 feet into the funnel which serves

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:14.420
as one of the cave's main exits. At the top, there is a gaping hole. And now the bats are

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:27.840
preparing to leave. They've assembled in a relatively small chamber close to the exit

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:34.760
and are flying round and round in a great swirling cloud waiting for daylight to fade.

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:45.280
And now, off they go.

00:39:45.280 --> 00:40:06.040
Behind us...

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:10.040
.

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:27.520
This brief hour of dusk is the moment when the two communities, the day fliers and the

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:33.000
night fliers, may encounter one another in the air.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:40.400
Outside danger awaits. Hunters belonging to that other great group of animals with

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:45.320
which they share the skies. Birds.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:51.320
Hawks, eagles and kites.

00:40:51.320 --> 00:41:04.120
They are why the bats were reluctant to leave and why they now do so in one continuous torrent.

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:06.360
There is safety in numbers.

00:41:06.360 --> 00:41:22.160
But some will pay the price.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:58.920
The vast majority, of course, make it out over the forest canopy.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:20.480
And there they can use that skill of echolocation to find food.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:26.080
The way that different animals have colonized the skies is surely one of the most remarkable

00:42:26.080 --> 00:42:34.960
stories in the natural world.

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:42.000
First to do so over 320 million years ago were the insects.

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:46.520
They had no competition for about 100 million years.

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:56.560
But then much larger flying animals took to the air, reptiles, the pterosaurs.

00:42:56.560 --> 00:43:02.920
Around 70 million years later still, one part of the dinosaurs acquired feathers and that

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:06.720
enabled their owners to get airborne.

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:09.360
The birds had arrived.

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:19.720
And last, about 60 million years ago, the night skies were invaded by mammals, the bats.

00:43:19.720 --> 00:43:28.080
And here in Comanton Cave, the three surviving groups of fliers, insects, birds and bats,

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:39.200
are still locked together in an ongoing evolutionary struggle.

00:43:39.200 --> 00:43:45.320
So the battle for the supremacy of the skies that started over 300 million years ago still

00:43:45.320 --> 00:44:12.560
continues every day around the world.
